+## Standalone computation catalog
+
+The PocketIC binary includes a computation catalog with its compiled charge-rule dictionary,
+runtime source excerpts and hashes, cost formulas and tips, default costs and limits, and
+explanations of the accounting units and limitations. Export it directly:
+
+```sh
+pocket-ic explain > computation-catalog.json
+pocket-ic explain --rule ic.memory.heap.access.v1
+pocket-ic explain --compact > computation-catalog.compact.json
+```
+
+The binary may also be named `pocket-ic-server`; the command is the same. `explain` writes
+only JSON to stdout and exits before creating a runtime, listener, port file or instance.
+It does not load or fetch a routing table. `--rule` selects one exact rule ID and preserves
+the rest of the catalog envelope; an unknown ID reports an error on stderr and exits nonzero.
+Server options such as `--port` and `--port-file` are separate from this command.
+`catalog_sha256` hashes the catalog's compact JSON content excluding that field itself.
+Filtered output recomputes this hash and preserves the complete catalog's hash as
+`full_catalog_sha256`.
+
+The exported JSON is self-contained, including rule implementation excerpts, so an agent does
+not need separate Markdown files. Its defaults describe this binary, not an instantiated
+subnet's current configuration or measured work. Use a trace's `runtime_configurations` and
+observed amounts when explaining a particular run. Guest source mapping still requires the
+matching module and external SDK/debug artifacts.
+
+A running PocketIC server exposes the same catalog at `GET /computation_catalog`; the viewer
+exposes it at `GET /api/computation-catalog` and through the MCP tool `get_computation_catalog`.
+These reads do not require an instance.
+
+## Diagnostic memory snapshots and byte images
+
+The web viewer's Memory workspace provides heap/stable page coverage and a Byte
+snapshots view with explicit before/after captures. Data, Reads and Writes use the
+same page order; Fit shows an overview and 1:1 shows one byte per CSS pixel.
+Compute and Executions have separate tabs, with the selected execution and bottom
+timeline shared across workspaces. Compute's Blocks tab adds a cost-weighted map
+of retained prepaid blocks linked to their exact rows. Full metadata remains in
+the inspector's Details tab. Offline trace exports show access evidence; they do
+not contain committed byte contents for snapshots.
+
+The server can retain immutable heap/stable PageMaps at explicit operation boundaries and
+render bounded byte windows as PNGs. This is a host diagnostic feature, separate from IC
+management snapshots: it runs no canister code and adds no IC instruction or cycle charge.
+Capture before and after the workload boundary you intend to inspect. A synchronous operation
+may include multiple messages/callbacks; ticks can add other work. Captures represent committed
+state, so a discarded attempt's working memory is not exposed as the next snapshot.
+
+For example, a Rust test can invoke the standard-library-only Python helper around a call:
+
+```rust
+use std::process::Command;
+
+// `pic`, `canister_id` and `payload` have already been initialized.
+let capture = |label: &str, output: &str| {
+    assert!(Command::new("python3")
+        .arg("rs/pocket_ic_server/src/profiling/viewer/memory_access_viewer.py")
+        .arg("snapshot")
+        .arg("--server").arg(pic.get_server_url().as_str())
+        .arg("--instance").arg(pic.instance_id().to_string())
+        .arg("--canister").arg(canister_id.to_text())
+        .arg("--label").arg(label).arg("-o").arg(output)
+        .status().unwrap().success());
+};
+pic.start_memory_access_tracing(canister_id);
+capture("before-call", "before.json");
+pic.update_call(canister_id, candid::Principal::anonymous(), "work", payload).unwrap();
+capture("after-call", "after.json");
+let viewer_url = pic.start_memory_access_viewer(None);
+println!("Image API and MCP: {viewer_url}");
+```
+
+Run from the IC repository root or adjust the script path. The capture command writes a
+metadata receipt containing `snapshot_id`; memory stays on the server. Retrieve a byte window
+using the viewer's separate URL and the two IDs:
+
+```sh
+memory_access_viewer.py image --viewer http://127.0.0.1:8090/ \
+  --snapshot-id <after-uuid> --compare-to <before-uuid> --memory both -o call.png
+memory_access_viewer.py snapshot-delete --viewer http://127.0.0.1:8090/ --snapshot-id <before-uuid>
+memory_access_viewer.py snapshot-delete --viewer http://127.0.0.1:8090/ --snapshot-id <after-uuid>
+```
+
+`image` saves the PNG and `call.json` sidecar. The default contact sheet includes all retained
+64 KiB blocks touched in the capture interval, from both heap and stable memory. Each block has
+256×256 byte pixels and an H:page/S:page ID; thin reserved magenta grid lines separate them.
+No legend or descriptive text is printed in the PNG: pass its JSON RGB palette separately to
+the viewing LLM. Use `--attempt-id` or `--logical-execution-id` for one call or installation.
+More than 256 blocks is explicitly paginated with `--page-offset` and JSON `more`/next offset.
+A manual window remains available with `--selection range --memory heap|stable --start-page N`.
+Difference colors show changed values, not all writes: same-value writes and write-then-restore
+remain invisible in the diff. Touch selection independently includes read-only blocks, and stable
+bytemap evidence includes direct first reads. Check coverage flags for missing/truncated attempts.
+New allocation is distinct from preexisting zeros; upgrade comparisons preserve module identities.
+
+For a read-frequency mask, replace `pic.start_memory_access_tracing(canister_id)` above with
+`pic.start_read_profiling(canister_id, 1)` before the first capture and workload. Export a second
+image with the same IDs and selection, adding `--view reads -o reads.png`. Its page positions
+match the data image exactly. It contains logical byte reads without memory values: cyan heap,
+green direct stable, orange native stable, with fixed logarithmic brightness for repeats.
+The JSON retains exact counters, path coverage and explicit truncation/saturation flags.
+The mode covers loads and copy/API source buffers, and adds host diagnostic work. Read intensity
+does not imply repeated native page taxes: those are first-access charges, exported separately.
+Stable direct reads avoid the stable page surcharge, while API/copy and heap destination costs
+remain. Existing traces without read profiling cannot reconstruct this mask.
+
+For writes alone, use `pic.start_write_profiling(canister_id, 1)` before the call and export
+`image --view writes -o writes.png`. For matching read and write masks from the same execution,
+use `pic.start_memory_profiling(canister_id, 1, true, true)`. The CLI also supports
+`start --read-mask --write-mask --max-executions 1`. Write masks show completed stores and
+copy/API destinations, including same-value and write-then-restore operations. Pink is heap;
+orange-red is stable; fixed logarithmic brightness shows frequency. Every mask preserves the
+same selected page positions and carries exact counters/coverage in its JSON sidecar.
+
+Agents can call MCP `capture_memory_snapshot` with instance/canister/label, then
+`get_memory_snapshot_image` with after/before IDs and optional call/install identity. The latter returns
+an actual MCP PNG image block plus text summary. `delete_memory_snapshot` releases the capture.
+The JSON/PNG interfaces are documented at `<viewer-url>/llms.txt`. The core REST capture route is
+`POST /instances/<instance>/read/capture_memory_snapshot` with base64 `canister_id` and optional
+`label`; no new typed Rust-client method is required for this workflow.
+
+Eight captures share a 128 MiB estimated retained-delta budget across the server. The estimate
+is not process RSS or an IC charge. Delete captures when done and export useful images before
+server restart: metadata receipts alone cannot reconstruct memory afterward.
+
+## Memory access tracing
+
+The replica charges canisters for every Wasm page (64 KiB) of the heap that a message execution accesses
+(reads or writes) and again for every page that it dirties (writes).
+To find out which pages the executions of a canister touch, PocketIC can record the accessed and dirty pages
+of every message execution of the canister, i.e., of update and query calls, response callbacks,
+timers, heartbeats, and canister installation and upgrades:
+
+```rust
+    pic.start_memory_access_tracing(canister_id);
+    // Run the workload, e.g., update calls, timers, ...
+    pic.update_call(canister_id, sender, "transfer", payload).unwrap();
+    let trace = pic.get_memory_access_trace(canister_id);
+    for execution in &trace.executions {
+        println!(
+            "{} {}: {} instructions, accessed {:?}, dirtied {:?}",
+            execution.api_type,
+            execution.function,
+            execution.instructions,
+            execution.accessed_pages,
+            execution.dirty_pages,
+        );
+    }
+    pic.stop_memory_access_tracing(canister_id);
+```
+
+The pages are reported as sorted ranges `[start, end)` of Wasm page indices, and `page_access_instructions` records
+raw heap access/dirty debits from the actual memory-fee callbacks. The recorded executions are kept
+in the RAM of the PocketIC server until `PocketIc::clear_memory_access_trace` is called (stopping the tracing keeps them).
+Dirty pages are not tracked for executions whose changes are discarded, e.g., for (non-replicated) queries.
+
+`instructions` is the runtime's final reported charged instruction total, not a count of Wasm operators or a CPU-time
+measurement. Raw heap fees contribute to this total in successful executions without terminal budget clamping.
+On an instruction-limit failure, raw heap fees can exceed the final reported total; consult the profile ledger's
+raw debits and identified terminal-budget adjustment. Subtracting `page_access_instructions` does not isolate computation:
+stable-memory charges, system API fees, and other runtime work remain. A traced heap page is 64 KiB; this differs
+from the memory tracker's 4 KiB operating-system granules.
+
+For internal instruction hotspots, select a limited execution window:
+
+```rust
+    pic.start_instruction_profiling(canister_id, 20);
+    // Run the workload to profile.
+    pic.update_call(canister_id, sender, "transfer", payload).unwrap();
+    let trace = pic.get_memory_access_trace(canister_id);
+```
+
+`max_executions` must be between 1 and 10000. Profiling detail is bounded and collected outside canister memory;
+ordinary memory access tracing can run without enabling instruction profiling. Profiling also enables memory tracing;
+when the selection is exhausted, memory tracing continues. `stop_memory_access_tracing` stops both. Executions carry
+`module_hash` and `module_hash_kind` so that upgrades and different builds can be distinguished.
+The trace's `modules` map stores original operator/block dictionaries once per installed-module hash, including
+function-body boundaries and the Code payload offset. Attach source artifacts only to a matching hash of
+the installed, uncompressed Wasm; a compressed-byte hash is insufficient. Profiles describe IC instruction charges,
+including prepaid metering blocks: a charged block does not prove that all its operators ran before a trap.
+Rust source lines, inline frames, and dynamic caller paths require further collection and matching build artifacts;
+flat block counts cannot reconstruct them. Source artifact creation and symbolization are separate from PocketIC.
+Check profile `truncated`, `limitations`, and `ledger.reconciled` before interpreting a ranking. An unexplained ledger
+difference stays visible instead of being assigned to source code. Each physical execution has an `attempt` with a
+server-scoped `attempt_id`, completion/abort status and bounded DTS slice records. Resumes retain that identity;
+retries have different attempt IDs and share an authoritative logical execution ID when supplied by the runtime.
+`disposition` reports actual replica state commitment separately from the Wasm reply/reject/trap result. Its logical
+ID joins the trace's `logical_executions` dictionary; unavailable correlations remain absent.
+An aborted paused attempt can retain a partial observer ledger without a final memory snapshot or instruction
+counter. Check `attempt.memory_details_available` and `attempt.instructions_available`; false means unavailable,
+not zero. `attempt.total_round_instructions` and slice rows measure scheduler work, separate from the guest ledger.
+The slice sum remains exact when bounded slice detail is truncated. Complete cross-canister causal graphs and guest
+Rust source/inline symbolization still require additional artifacts.
+
+The final module origin map has a separate compilation-time limit, defaulting to
+262,144 ranges. For a large Wasm module, launch a fresh server with larger bounded
+module and trace budgets before installing or loading the canister:
+
+```sh
+pocket-ic --instruction-profile-max-origin-ranges 1048576 \
+  --memory-access-trace-limit-mib 256
+```
+
+Rust and other clients that launch the server can pass the same settings through
+their environment:
+
+```sh
+POCKET_IC_INSTRUCTION_PROFILE_MAX_ORIGIN_RANGES=1048576 \
+POCKET_IC_MEMORY_ACCESS_TRACE_LIMIT_MIB=256 \
+POCKET_IC_BIN="$HOME/.local/bin/pocket-ic" cargo test -- --nocapture
+```
+
+These are host diagnostic limits; instruction charges and canister limits are
+unchanged. The trace budget defaults to 64 MiB per instance and includes shared
+module dictionaries; it is a conservative retained-size estimate, not an RSS cap.
+Larger module maps can require a larger trace budget too. Restart the server so
+the module is recompiled under the new origin-map setting; shortening the execution
+capture window cannot repair a module map that was already truncated at compilation.
+
+The module dictionary's `origin_map_truncated` and each profile's
+`module_origin_map_truncated` report incomplete module coordinate maps.
+`module_origin_map_unavailable` reports missing or omitted dictionaries, including
+retention-budget omissions. `stack_details_truncated` reports execution stack
+capture limits independently. An unmapped frame can therefore have a complete
+physical stack but incomplete source attribution. Check all these fields before
+claiming complete attribution; execution totals and page accounting can still reconcile.
+
+To understand a charge, join each `instruction_profile.rules` row's `rule_id` to the trace's shared `charge_rules`
+dictionary, or open **Rules** in the viewer. Definitions explain the condition, formula, and an optimization
+opportunity, with the actual runtime source excerpt and full source-file hash. These are runtime accounting rules,
+separate from guest Rust source mapping. Rule totals are a projection of the ledger; do not add them to it again.
+Some rows record observed path decisions with zero instruction charges. Tips are opportunities to investigate,
+not guaranteed savings: page first-access/dirty fees depend on execution state and earlier accesses.
+
+The JSON trace format is versioned. Version 2 emits 64-bit counters and page indices as decimal strings, while the
+Rust API still uses `u64` and accepts legacy numeric JSON. Keep these values as integers when processing exports;
+converting them to JavaScript `Number` can lose precision. Export traces before restarting the server: they live in RAM.
+Retention limits are reported by trace `truncated`, `dropped_executions`, and execution `detail_truncated`; omitted page
+detail must not be read as zero accesses. `dropped_instructions` preserves the instruction sum of omitted completed
+records as a decimal-string `u128`; it cannot be attributed to filters without those records.
+Stopping or clearing does not cancel an execution already in flight, which
+can finish and be recorded afterward.
+`dropped_instructions_incomplete: true` means this omitted-attempt sum is only a known subtotal:
+an instruction counter was unavailable or the aggregate saturated. Do not claim full reconciliation.
+Retained attempts independently report counter availability in `attempt.instructions_available`.
+
+The PocketIC server can show the traces of all its instances in a web app while they are recorded:
+
+```rust
+    let url = pic.start_memory_access_viewer(None);
+    println!("{url}#{}:{canister_id}", pic.instance_id());
+```
+
+Alternatively, set the environment variable `POCKET_IC_MEMORY_ACCESS_VIEWER_PORT` (e.g., to `8090`) when running
+the tests, which makes the PocketIC server serve the viewer at http://127.0.0.1:8090/.
+The web app plays back the executions on a bitmap of the heap with one square per accessed page (in groups of
+nearby pages), zooms into any range of pages, shows which executions ran in the same block, and lists statistics per
+function. The viewer keeps the PocketIC server alive while it is open, so that the
+traces of finished tests can still be explored.
+
+Agents can download and analyze the traces through a JSON API and an MCP server described at `<url>/llms.txt`,
+e.g., after adding the MCP server to Claude Code with `claude mcp add --transport http pocket-ic-memory <url>mcp`.
+
+The script `rs/pocket_ic_server/src/profiling/viewer/memory_access_viewer.py` in the [IC repository](https://github.com/dfinity/ic)
+turns a trace into an interactive web page and into a video:
+
+```rust
+    std::fs::write("trace.json", serde_json::to_vec(&trace).unwrap()).unwrap();
+```
+
+```sh
+memory_access_viewer.py html trace.json -o report.html
+memory_access_viewer.py video trace.json -o trace.mp4 --duration 60
+```
+
+## Whole-system profiling
+
+Run `pocket-ic explain` for the complete compiled profiling manual and command
+inventory, or `pocket-ic profile explain` for the system component/measurement
+dictionary. Neither requires Markdown files or a running server. The shared
+implementation and agent tools live under `rs/pocket_ic_server/src/profiling/`.
+
+System profiling measures actual host CPU, resident memory, storage accounting,
+component execution scopes, logical scheduling events and observed inter-canister
+flows across a workload. It does not change IC instruction charges or implicitly
+enable detailed instruction/byte-mask instrumentation.
+
+```rust
+use pocket_ic::{PocketIcBuilder, SystemProfileConfig, SystemProfileQuery,
+                SystemProfileStopOptions};
+
+let pic = PocketIcBuilder::new().with_application_subnet().build();
+// Install/fund canisters first if setup should be excluded.
+let capture = pic.start_system_profile(SystemProfileConfig {
+    name: "multi-canister workload".into(),
+    sample_interval_ms: 50,
+    ..Default::default()
+});
+let run_id = capture["run_id"].as_str().unwrap();
+
+// Run ordinary pic.update_call/query_call/tick operations here.
+pic.tick();
+
+let finished = pic.stop_system_profile_with_options(run_id,
+    SystemProfileStopOptions { drain_ms: 100, reason: Some("workload complete".into()) });
+let components = pic.query_system_profile(run_id, "components",
+    &SystemProfileQuery { limit: 100, ..Default::default() });
+std::fs::write("run.icprofile", pic.export_system_profile(run_id)).unwrap();
+let query_id = components["query_id"].as_str().unwrap();
+std::fs::write("components.png",
+    pic.render_system_profile(run_id, query_id, "components", "png")).unwrap();
+println!("{}", finished);
+println!("{}", pic.start_memory_access_viewer(None));
+// pic.delete_system_profile(run_id) releases a stopped retained capture.
+```
+
+The nonblocking client has the same async methods. `get_system_profile` returns
+a small summary; `query_system_profile` selects `status`, `summary`, `components`,
+`resources`, `events`, `flows`, `limits`, `rounds`, `pricing`, `metrics`, or `explain`. Counter values
+use exact decimal strings and unsupported measurements are null with coverage
+reasons. Sample resolution and retained event limits are configurable.
+
+Existing IC metrics default to `metrics_level: "standard"`: raw counter/gauge
+values, labels, units, and histogram count/sum remain available. Use `"full"`
+to retain bounded raw histogram buckets and summary quantiles, or `"none"` to
+skip registry gathering with explicit disabled coverage. With no drain, metrics
+are captured at initial and stop boundaries; a positive drain adds a distinct
+foreground-end snapshot. Omitted buckets are null with `buckets_collected=false`,
+not empty distributions. `memory_detail_interval_ms` defaults to1000 and includes
+boundary PSS readings; zero disables all PSS mapping scans, including boundaries.
+CPU, RSS and available I/O counters remain enabled in that mode.
+The CLI exposes both options as `--metrics-level` and
+`--memory-detail-interval-ms`.
+
+Dedicated scheduler evidence is available with `query_system_profile(run_id,
+"rounds", &SystemProfileQuery { subnet_id: Some(subnet), round_details: Some(false), limit: 1000,
+..Default::default() })`, CLI `pocket-ic profile rounds`, and MCP
+`profile_rounds`. Use `round_details: Some(false)` for many-round overview rows,
+then `round_id: Some(selected_id), round_details: Some(true), limit: 1` for one
+full timeline. Default None/true preserves full detail. Query projection marks
+`details_omitted_by_query` and retained-detail counts without changing collected
+source completeness or totals; page full detail conservatively for large runs.
+Each rounds response includes `rounds.scheduler_model`, the same explanation
+provided by `pocket-ic profile explain` and the embedded system dictionary in
+`pocket-ic explain`. Eligible canisters are assigned to per-worker lists before
+each inner iteration. Workers process their lists sequentially, with multiple
+messages/slices possible per canister, and join at a barrier without work
+stealing. After collecting/finalizing results and inducting local messages if
+continuing, the scheduler can build another iteration. Each iteration charges
+its maximum worker consumption plus finalization, not summed worker work or
+necessarily one physical thread's cumulative instructions.
+
+For `R=max_instructions_per_round` and
+`S=max(update_slice,install_code_slice)`, the dispatch counter begins at
+`R-S+1`. It is checked before starting a slice, leaving room for that slice to
+finish after the counter crosses zero. Illustrative application defaults are
+40B per message, 2B per slice, 4B nominal per worker round, four workers and a
+2,000,000,001 initial counter. Approximately 16B aggregate nominal capacity
+requires busy, balanced workers and is not a shared pool or exact hard ceiling.
+There is no hard 2B canister-round quota. The round chart divides accumulated
+iteration maxima plus finalization by the nominal 4B, so roughly 50% can already
+be near the dispatch gate. Actual captured configuration, overheads, reserve
+use and heap-delta/other stopping reasons determine the interpretation.
+
+The result contains exact round/iteration/worker/canister/slice
+rows, captured configuration versions, stopping reasons and independent quality.
+`round_id` and `round_number` select exact rounds; `canister_id` selects whole
+rounds containing a retained matching pass while preserving global budgets.
+Dedicated records survive generic event detail loss within their own bounded
+retention; inspect dedicated dropped/rejected/censored and omitted-detail fields.
+Older imports explicitly lack this evidence rather than reconstructing it from
+incomplete events. Nominal round allowance, reserved initial counter, sum of
+parallel worker work, max-worker global decrement, per-message cap and DTS slice
+cap have different scopes. Whole-message totals can include earlier rounds.
+Nullable `heap_delta_bytes` records the scheduler logical dirty-memory proxy,
+not RSS or actual disk writes. The captured `max_heap_delta_per_iteration_bytes`
+threshold is checked against round-cumulative delta between iterations; it can
+stop a round before instruction exhaustion. Old captures keep null/unknown
+values and summary known/unknown round counts remain explicit.
+A declared application turn budget is not an IC slice limit. PNG/SVG/JSON
+`view=rounds` preserves the same immutable query identity and exact sidecar.
+
+The viewer's **System** workspace provides Overview, Calls, Components, Resources
+and Pricing views and preserves the existing Memory/Compute/Executions drilldowns.
+It can capture a run before any canister traces exist. Component CPU scopes,
+exclusive CPU and wall intervals are separate; wait time is not CPU use.
+
+Physical resources cover the observed server/process tree. On a shared server,
+an instance selection filters logical evidence but cannot exactly divide shared
+process memory or background CPU. Summed RSS is explicitly not unique physical
+RAM; PSS/cgroup measurements have their own sources. Storage accounting is not
+the same as application read bytes or completed device I/O. Network wire bytes
+remain unavailable unless an appropriate collector observes them.
+
+PocketIC executes real scheduling, execution and some XNet stream processing,
+but replaces real consensus/P2P transport and some cryptography. Direct calls
+bypass envelope authentication; the gateway path can perform real verification.
+The report states actual component coverage. A bounded drain is an observation
+window, not proof that every checkpoint or kernel writeback is complete.
+
+The installed executable also supports:
+
+```sh
+pocket-ic profile start --server http://127.0.0.1:PORT --name workload
+pocket-ic profile summary --server http://127.0.0.1:PORT --run RUN_ID
+pocket-ic profile components --server http://127.0.0.1:PORT --run RUN_ID
+pocket-ic profile rounds --server http://127.0.0.1:PORT --run RUN_ID --query '{"limit":1000,"round_details":false}'
+pocket-ic profile stop --server http://127.0.0.1:PORT --run RUN_ID --drain-ms 100
+pocket-ic profile export --server http://127.0.0.1:PORT --run RUN_ID --output run.icprofile
+pocket-ic profile render --input run.icprofile --view components --format png --output components.png
+pocket-ic profile compare --input before.icprofile --other-input after.icprofile
+pocket-ic profile explain
+```
+
+For real-process observation, `profile capture --pid PID --duration 10 --output
+run.icprofile` attaches without stopping the target. `profile record --backend
+replica --duration 30 --output run.icprofile -- /path/to/instrumented-replica ...`
+launches a command with the profiling transport. Capture starts after spawn;
+earlier startup activity is not observed. At the duration limit, record stops its
+owned command process group unless `--keep-command-running` is provided. It does
+not stop independently attached processes. Additional service roots can be named
+with repeated `--extra-pid` options. Host-only attachment does not invent native
+component spans for a binary that lacks active instrumentation.
+Workload stdout is routed to stderr, keeping the profiler's stdout valid JSON.
+
+Both the native server and viewer expose `/api/profiling/runs`; the viewer's MCP
+endpoint includes `profile_start`, `profile_summary`, `profile_components`,
+`profile_resources`, `profile_flows`, `profile_limits`, `profile_pricing`,
+`profile_evidence`, `profile_compare`, `profile_render`, `profile_export` and
+`profile_explain`. Images and JSON share immutable query IDs; stale retained
+query IDs fail explicitly instead of silently rendering a newer measurement.
