+  caches on Linux tmpfs, preserving normal instruction and cycle accounting.
+  `--ram-dir` selects another executable-capable tmpfs; `/storage` reports the mode.
+- Physical attempt IDs, bounded DTS slice records and exact round-work totals, including aborted paused attempts.
+  Snapshot availability and authoritative commit/discard disposition remain separate from the Wasm result;
+  runtime logical execution context links retry attempts without inventing missing causal relationships.
+- `pocket-ic explain` exports a self-contained compiled computation catalog as JSON without starting
+  a runtime or server. It includes rule/source explanations, costs, default limits and accounting
+  limitations; exact `--rule` filtering and `--compact` output are supported. The same catalog is
+  available at `/computation_catalog`, the viewer's `/api/computation-catalog`, and MCP `get_computation_catalog`.
+- Bounded physical Wasm call paths, exact final-instrumented-code origin ranges, and signal-safe native fault-PC
+  evidence for memory debits. The viewer exposes Origins/Call paths and weighted Speedscope export; unmapped
+  intervals and truncated detail remain explicit. Matching SDK/debug artifacts are required for Rust source/inline views.
+- Host accounting alongside Wasm instruction profiles: actual cycle charges, execution/transport prepayments and refunds,
+  attached transfers, storage reservations, native management work, scheduler/query overhead and HTTP settlements.
+  Independent runtime pagination, exact large totals, guarded clearing, source rule explanations and captured limits
+  are available through the viewer JSON/MCP APIs and offline exports. Host costs and Limits have separate viewer tabs.
+- Memory access tracing: the endpoints `/instances/<instance_id>/update/start_memory_access_tracing` and
+  `/instances/<instance_id>/update/stop_memory_access_tracing` start and stop recording, in RAM, which Wasm pages (64 KiB)
+  of the heap every message execution of a canister (e.g., update and query calls, response callbacks, timers, heartbeats,
+  and canister installation) accessed (read or written) and dirtied (wrote), as charged by the memory tracker.
+  The endpoint `/instances/<instance_id>/read/get_memory_access_trace` returns the recorded executions together with
+  their type, function, call context, time, instructions, and outcome, and the endpoint
+  `/instances/<instance_id>/update/clear_memory_access_trace` deletes them.
+  The script `memory_access_viewer/memory_access_viewer.py` controls the recording and turns recorded traces into
+  an interactive HTML page or a video.
+- The memory access viewer: a web app showing the memory access traces of all instances while they are recorded,
+  served on a separate port if the server is started with `--memory-access-viewer-port <port>` or the environment
+  variable `POCKET_IC_MEMORY_ACCESS_VIEWER_PORT`, or on demand by the endpoint `/memory_access_viewer`.
+  It also serves a JSON API (described at `/llms.txt`) and an MCP server (at `/mcp`) for agents to list, download,
+  and analyze the traces and to control tracing. Requests to the viewer keep the server alive.
+- `POST /instances/<instance_id>/update/start_instruction_profiling` selects a bounded number of executions for
+  instruction charge profiling. Its JSON body takes a base64-encoded `canister_id` and optional `max_executions`
+  (default 20, range 1–10000). Profiles are returned through the existing memory access trace API with an
+  installed-module hash and its hash kind. Memory access tracing can still run without instruction profiling.
+- Recorded runtime rule explanations in the viewer and trace API: charge conditions, formulas, optimization
+  opportunities, and compiled runtime source excerpts/hashes. Rule totals are another view of existing debits,
+  with observed zero-charge path decisions retained separately from instruction charges.
+
+### Changed
+- Version 2 memory access traces encode 64-bit counters and page indices as decimal strings. Legacy numeric input
+  remains accepted. Rust source locations and inline frames require matching external source artifacts; physical Wasm stacks and available operation origins are collected separately.
